This is the exercism Go "connect" (Hex) problem. Standard solution:

- Parse the board into a 2D grid. The board is a rhombus (parallelogram) drawn as a triangular representation with leading spaces. After stripping spaces, line i has length (n - i), where n is the board size (number of columns in the top row).
- O plays top to bottom (connects row 0 to the last row). X plays left to right (connects the left edge to the right edge).
- Adjacent cells: in the rhombus representation, cell (row, col) is adjacent to (row+1, col), (row+1, col+1), (row, col+1), (row-1, col), (row-1, col-1), (row, col-1).
- O wins if there's a path from the top row to the bottom row. X wins if there's a path from the left edge (col 0) to the right edge (col = len(row)-1).
- It's impossible for both to win on a valid board. But if both are found... in the exercism tests, only one wins. Standard approach: check O first, or check X first — the canonical solution checks one or the other. In fact, it's impossible for both to win in a valid Hex game, but with an arbitrary board both could connect. The tests don't include a case where both win. In the standard exercism Go solution: check O first (top to bottom), and if found return "O", otherwise check X.

